    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar Lodi</a> rebuilt Agra in 1504–1505 and made it his seat of government, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> took it after defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a> at Panipat in 1526.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> built the modern city on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> and the Agra Fort, and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal</a> there from 1632.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1658, after which Agra passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, and it became a district town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> went to find a site for a garden at Agra, he wrote in his memoir that the country was so ugly and detestable that he crossed back over the river repulsed; he then planted gardens in every corner of the spot he had chosen.</p></aside>
    </section>

