    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1504</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar Lodi</a> rebuilds Agra and makes it the seat of government</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1526</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a> at Panipat and takes Agra</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1565</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> commissions the Agra Fort</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1632</span><span class="rf-text">Construction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal</a> begins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1658</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> moves the entire court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1761</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suraj-mal">Suraj Mal</a> conquer Agra</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1803</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> takes the city after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-maratha-war">Second Anglo-Maratha War</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1857</span><span class="rf-text">Agra becomes one of the centres of the Indian rebellion</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/akbar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akbar.jpg" alt="Akbar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Govardhan._Akbar_With_Lion_and_Calf_ca._1630,_Metmuseum_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Govardhan / Mir Ali Heravi, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Agra stands on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, on a level plain about 230 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in the Braj region. Several derivations of its name have been proposed, none very satisfactory. The most accepted is the Hindi word agar, a salt pan, given because of the region's brackish soil, where salt was once made by evaporation. Others derive it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> agra, 'front', meaning the first of the small forests where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> played with the gopis of Vrindavan.</p><p>Agra has two histories. The first is that of an ancient city on the east, or left, bank of the Yamuna, which goes back so far that it is lost in the legends of Krishna and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>. The second is that of the modern city, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> in 1558 on the right bank. Little remains of the ancient city except a few traces of its foundations. It was a place of importance under various <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> dynasties before the Muslim invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, but its history is unclear. The 17th-century chronicler Abdullah said it was a village before the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar Lodi</a>. The king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> had used the Agra fort as a jail, and the site was degraded by the destruction <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> brought upon it. Masud Sa'd Salman claimed to have been there when Mahmud assaulted Agra, and said that the Raja Japal surrendered after seeing a nightmare. Mahmud nevertheless pillaged the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi.jpg" alt="Sikandar Lodi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar Lodi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Sikandar_Lodi_028.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anupamg, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Agra's period of historical importance began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar Lodi</a>. In 1504–1505 this Afghan ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> rebuilt the city and made it the seat of government. A commission he appointed inspected both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> to Etawah and chose a place on the left bank. The city grew into a large town with royal presence, officials, merchants, scholars, theologians and artists, and became one of the most important centres of Islamic learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> Agra was the foremost city of the subcontinent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> built the modern city on the right bank, connected it with the parts of his empire and raised the ramparts of the Agra Fort, and made Agra a centre of learning, arts, commerce and religion. Before his death it had become probably one of the biggest cities in the east, with a great deal of trade in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a>. The English traveller Ralph Fitch, who visited in September 1585, called Agra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatehpur-sikri">Fatehpur Sikri</a> two very great cities, each much greater than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> wrote that the city's buildings equalled several cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, Khurasan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> put together and that many people had built houses of three or four storeys. Jahangir loved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> more than Agra, but it remained the first city of the realm. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>'s building raised it to the pinnacle of its glory.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/babur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babur.jpg" alt="Babur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Humayun_and_Babur_(Late_Shah_Jahan_Album)_Babur_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">c.1650, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar Lodi</a>, who reigned from 1489 to 1517, made Agra the seat of government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. After his death in 1517 the city passed to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a>, who ruled from Agra until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> defeated and killed him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-panipat">First Battle of Panipat</a> in 1526. Babur sent his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> ahead, and Humayun occupied the town without opposition. Babur was determined to make Agra the seat of his government. Humayun was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kanauj</a> in 1539 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a>, whose short-lived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sur-empire">Sur Empire</a> held the region from 1540 to 1556, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> reconquered it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-panipat">Second Battle of Panipat</a> in 1556.</p><p>Agra was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> under Babur, Humayun, Akbar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, until 1658, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> moved the whole court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. In official correspondence Agra continued to be called the second capital of the empire. As Mughal power declined, control of the city passed in the late 18th century to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, the Mughals again, the ruler of Gwalior and finally the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. The Jats under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suraj-mal">Suraj Mal</a> conquered it on 12 June 1761 after a month-long siege, and held it until 1774, when the Mughal commander Mirza Najaf Khan recaptured it. The Marathas took it in 1785 and the East India Company in 1803, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-maratha-war">Second Anglo-Maratha War</a>. Agra was the capital of the short-lived Presidency of Agra in 1834–1836 and of the North-Western Province from 1836 to 1858.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taj-mahal.jpg" alt="Taj Mahal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taj_Mahal_(Edited).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yann; edited by Jim Carter, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Agra had a mixed population of courtiers, merchants, scholars and artists. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar Lodi</a> it was among the most important centres of Islamic learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> it was a centre of learning, arts, commerce and religion. Akbar founded the religion called Din-i-Ilahi, which is now extinct and which Agra is regarded as the birthplace of. The Radhaswami faith also began in Agra, and has about two million followers worldwide. The city's chronicles and travellers' accounts describe crowded lanes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> wrote that the mass of people was so great that moving about in them was difficult, and the traveller William Finch found the city populous beyond measure.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> laid out the first formal Mughal garden in India, the Aram Bagh, on the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>, planting roses and narcissus in beds that matched one another. Jahangir laid many gardens inside the Red Fort. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, who was known for his keen interest in architecture, craftsmen from India, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> worked on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal</a>, and pietra dura inlay work decorated its interiors. Marble inlay and carpets remained royal crafts of the city. After 1858 Agra was a major centre of Hindi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> journalism, and the Agra Lunatic Asylum opened in 1859. Agra College, founded in 1823 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> scholar Pandit Gangadhar Shastri, produced the first graduate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, and St Peter's College was founded in 1846 and St John's College in 1850.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni.jpg" alt="Mahmud of Ghazni" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahmud_of_Ghazni,_Ghaznavid_ruler,_conquering_Qasdar_(modern_Khuzdar)_in_India,_miniature_from_the_Jami%CA%BF_al-Tawarikh_of_Rashid_al-Din_Il-Khanid_Tabriz_Ms_Or_20_f.108v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1314-15, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> assaulted Agra and pillaged it. In the later 18th century, as the Mughal empire declined, the city changed hands repeatedly. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a> took it on 12 June 1761 after a month-long siege, they melted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> doors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal</a>. In 1688 the Jats under Rajaram Jat had damaged the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> at Sikandra to avenge his uncle Gokula Jat, who had been executed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>. They plundered its gold, jewels, silver and carpets, and burned Akbar's bones. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> was confined in the Agra Fort by his son Aurangzeb from 1658 until his death in 1666.</p><p>In the rebellion of 1857 the news of the mutiny at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meerut">Meerut</a> reached Agra on 14 May. Some companies of the 44th and 67th Native Infantry mutinied on 30 May and carried the treasury they had been sent to fetch from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> to the rebels in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and the rest of those battalions were disarmed on 31 May. The Gwalior contingent mutinied on 15 June, and all other native units followed. On 3 July some 6,000 Europeans and associated people moved into the Agra Fort, and on 5 July a British force that attacked approaching mutineers was defeated and retreated into the fort. Despite an uprising by a mob and extreme disorder in the city, the British restored partial order by 8 July. After Delhi fell in September, a brigade under Edward Greathed reached Agra on 11 October and defeated a surprise attack, an action named the Battle of Agra. The capital of the North-Western Provinces was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> in 1858, and Agra declined to a provincial town.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/islam-shah-suri"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/islam-shah-suri.jpg" alt="Islam Shah Suri" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/islam-shah-suri">Islam Shah Suri</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Islam_Shah_Suri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Agra's monuments are mostly from its Mughal past. The Agra Fort, a fortress of red sandstone with a perimeter of about 2.5 kilometres, was commissioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> in 1565 and took around eight years to build, on the site of earlier fortifications by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam-shah-suri">Islam Shah Suri</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> changed it considerably, and Shah Jahan added marble buildings including the Moti Masjid and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">Diwan</a>-i-Khas. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal</a> was built for Shah Jahan's wife Mumtaz Mahal, who died in childbirth in 1631. Its chief architect was probably the Persian Ustad Ahmad Lahori. Construction began in 1632 with upwards of twenty thousand workers, the mausoleum was finished by 1639 and the adjunct buildings by 1643, and the whole complex took 22 years. Shah Jahan's own tomb stands beside hers.</p><p>Akbar's tomb at Sikandra was completed by Jahangir in 1613, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-jahan">Nur Jahan</a> commissioned the tomb of I'timad-ud-Daulah, her father and Jahangir's chief minister, whose white marble and pietra dura presage the Taj Mahal. The Jama Masjid was built in 1648. Akbar's capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatehpur-sikri">Fatehpur Sikri</a>, about 35 kilometres from Agra, was later abandoned. The Taj Mahal and the Agra Fort became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> in 1983.</p><p>Agra is today a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, the fourth most populous of the state, with 1,585,704 people at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It has been part of Uttar Pradesh since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> in 1947, and has developed into an industrial city, with leather and footwear manufacture.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Agra, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, was rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar Lodi</a> in 1504–1505 and was the capital of the Mughal emperors until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1658. It holds the Agra Fort and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal</a>, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>.</em></p>

